# The stable vanity URLs. Cloudflare's static-assets runtime reads this file from
# the build output; Astro copies public/ into dist/ verbatim.
#
# 302 throughout, not 301: every destination here is one that is expected to
# move — a different host, a mirror, a new payment link — and a cached permanent
# redirect would outlive the decision. These URLs are the stable half; what they
# point at is not.

# The update feed. This one is not a convenience: SUFeedURL in Bastion-Info.plist
# is baked into every binary ever shipped, so it must be a URL that can be
# repointed without asking anyone to update first. `latest/download` also means
# publishing a release never needs a site deploy.
/appcast.xml  https://github.com/mgcrea/bastion/releases/latest/download/appcast.xml  302

# The one stable download URL, on the same reasoning. It points at
# `latest/download` rather than a versioned asset, which only works because the
# release asset is named Bastion.zip with no version in it.
/download     https://github.com/mgcrea/bastion/releases/latest/download/Bastion.zip  302
/checksum     https://github.com/mgcrea/bastion/releases/latest/download/Bastion.zip.sha256  302
/releases     https://github.com/mgcrea/bastion/releases  302

# The buy link. A vanity path for the same reason as the rest: the Stripe
# checkout URL is exactly the kind of destination that moves, and the app links
# to /buy from the Licence pane — a URL compiled into shipped binaries, which
# must never be a Stripe URL directly.
#
# The live payment link, created from the 1.x launch price. Its twin in test mode
# is the one to point at when exercising fulfilment end to end; swapping this line
# is the whole of that switch.
/buy          https://buy.stripe.com/aFaeVd4NW1tQdta0WOdZ601  302
